NOTHING RUNS THE SCRIPTS IN THE cron.* DIRECTORIES ON THIS SYSTEM YET.

These four directories are installed and empty:

    /etc/cron.hourly
    /etc/cron.daily
    /etc/cron.weekly
    /etc/cron.monthly

A script you put in one of them will not run. They are read by fcron, this
system's periodical command scheduler, and fcron.service ships DISABLED, with
no socket and no D-Bus activation — so nothing starts it and nothing reads
these directories until you turn it on:

    sudo systemctl enable --now fcron.service

After that, the system fcrontab runs run-parts over each directory on its
schedule: cron.hourly at 1 minute past every hour, cron.daily at 04:02,
cron.weekly at 04:22 on Sunday, and cron.monthly at 04:42 on the first of the
month. run-parts executes every file in the directory that is executable and
whose name has no extension it is told to ignore, so a script needs chmod +x
to run, and a leftover like backup.sh.bak is skipped rather than run twice.

To check whether it is on:

    systemctl is-enabled fcron.service

WHY IT SHIPS OFF. A job scheduler runs arbitrary commands as root or as the
submitting user on a timer, and this package also installs setuid binaries. A
machine that has been given no jobs to run does not need that running by
default. The decision is written down, with its full reasoning and the
consequence of reverting it, in the preset file that carries it:

    /usr/lib/systemd/system-preset/80-intergenos-enable.preset

That file is the single place the default is decided; turning fcron on by
default is a one-line change there. Enabling the unit on your own machine, as
above, does not touch it.

The other scheduler on this system, atd, ships off for the same reasons and is
described in the same file. Log rotation does NOT depend on either one — it
runs from logrotate.timer, which is enabled.
